(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-heart-failure-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Heart Failure Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 80-year-old man in the intensive care unit with acute heart failure has cool extremities and hypotension, and inotropic support is being considered. Which agents are used for this?","choices":["Spironolactone or eplerenone","Losartan or irbesartan","Dobutamine or milrinone","Diltiazem or verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy for intensive care use includes the sympathomimetics dobutamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine and the phosphodiesterase inhibitor milrinone. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had an ejection fraction of 25% two years ago. On guideline-directed therapy his repeat echocardiogram now shows 46%. How is his heart failure now classified?","choices":["Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction","Resolved heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fraction that was 40% or less and has recovered above 40% is improved ejection fraction. The number alone would read mildly reduced, but his history is what defines the group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with high-output heart failure from untreated hyperthyroidism has leg edema and breathlessness. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Start verapamil to slow the heart rate","Refer for heart transplant evaluation","Treat the thyroid disease and add a diuretic","Start dobutamine to support contractility"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is treated by correcting the underlying cause of increased demand, here hyperthyroidism, with a diuretic for congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has exertional breathlessness. Her electrocardiogram shows tall R waves in V5 and V6 and deep S waves in V1 and V2. What does this pattern show?","choices":["Right heart strain","Acute inferior injury","A normal tracing","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tall R waves in V5 and V6 with deep S waves in V1 and V2 indicate left ventricular hypertrophy, the structural change behind hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and the diastolic chain. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins) with a raised B-type natriuretic peptide. Right-sided heart failure is suspected. Which test establishes the definitive diagnosis?","choices":["Repeat natriuretic peptide","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Echocardiogram","Right heart catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clinical heart failure with distended neck veins raises suspicion of right-sided failure, and the echocardiogram gives the definitive diagnosis by ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction takes sacubitril-valsartan. He now has increasing breathlessness, and a natriuretic peptide test is planned. Which test is the more dependable choice?","choices":["The N-terminal precursor fragment","B-type natriuretic peptide","Cardiac troponin level","Complete blood count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is the choice: neprilysin degrades B-type natriuretic peptide, so an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor alters that result. Knowing which peptide was measured matters in these patients. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man comes to urgent care with two days of breathlessness. His B-type natriuretic peptide is 60. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Acute heart failure is ruled out","Heart failure is likely","The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A level below 100 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, so another cause of his breathlessness should be sought. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has progressive breathlessness and ankle edema over three months. Her N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 720. What does this level suggest for her age?","choices":["Heart failure is likely","Heart failure is ruled out","The level is normal for her age","Only a younger cut-off applies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older than 75, an N-terminal fragment above 450 makes heart failure likely. In a younger patient the threshold would be 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has increasing lethargy, episodes of near-syncope and cool extremities, without new edema. Which feature of the heart failure syndrome do these findings reflect?","choices":["Worsening pulmonary congestion","Portal venous overload","Systemic volume overload","Inadequate tissue perfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Weakness, fatigue, lethargy, malaise, syncope or near-syncope and obtundation are signs of inadequate tissue perfusion, the low-output side of the syndrome, distinct from congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 44%) has ankle edema and breathlessness. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation?","choices":["A diuretic as needed","An evidence-based beta blocker","A mineralocorticoid antagonist","A neprilysin combination drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In mildly reduced ejection fraction, diuretics as needed carry the one strong recommendation. The sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate; the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old African American man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 25%) remains short of breath with slight exertion despite optimized sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which addition is recommended?","choices":["Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Verapamil for additional rate control","Isosorbide mononitrate on its own","Dobutamine continued at home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For African American patients with reduced ejection fraction in functional class III or IV, hydralazine with nitrates carries a strong recommendation on top of the core classes. Slight-exertion symptoms put him in functional class III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman was hospitalized last year for heart failure with edema. On treatment she is now symptom-free and asks whether she has moved back to stage B. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["She remains in stage C","She is now in stage B","She is now in stage A","She has moved to stage D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage C is symptomatic structural heart disease, past or current. Once symptoms have occurred, resolution on treatment does not move the stage backward. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman wore a defibrillator vest for three months after an infarction, with optimized guideline-directed therapy and cardiac rehabilitation. Her repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 28%. She is class II and expected to live many years. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the vest indefinitely","Heart transplant evaluation now","Stop therapy and recheck in a year","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pathway ends with an implantable device when the repeat echocardiogram shows no improvement. She also meets the criteria: ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III, optimal therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with 20 years of poorly controlled hypertension has ankle swelling that has recently spread to crackles at both lung bases. Blood pressure is 176/98. A fourth heart sound (a sound in late diastole just before the first heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows thick left ventricular walls with an ejection fraction of 62%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systolic failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from thyroid excess","Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diastolic chain is complete: hypertension driving hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a fourth heart sound from atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, peripheral then pulmonary edema, and a preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension has never had cardiac symptoms. An echocardiogram shows left ventricular hypertrophy with preserved function. Which heart failure stage is he in?","choices":["Stage D","Stage B","Stage A","Stage C"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic structural heart disease, including left ventricular hypertrophy, is stage B, pre-heart failure. Stage A requires a structurally normal heart. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has progressive leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins). At the apex there is a low-pitched diastolic rumble with an opening snap. Which is the most likely cause of her right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Mitral valve stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic valve stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex with an opening snap is mitral stenosis, one of the three causes of right-sided failure with chronic left-sided failure and pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with heart failure has an echocardiogram reporting an ejection fraction of 44% and a multigated acquisition scan the same month reporting 32%. Which result should decide her ejection fraction group?","choices":["Whichever value is higher","The multigated acquisition scan","The echocardiogram","An average of the two"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The multigated acquisition scan is the most accurate study for ejection fraction, so its 32% places her in reduced ejection fraction. The echocardiogram is the one to trust for structures such as the valves. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man wore a defibrillator vest after an infarction while his guideline-directed therapy was optimized and he completed cardiac rehabilitation. His repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 47%. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start inotropic therapy at home","Continue therapy without a device","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","Continue the vest indefinitely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The implantable defibrillator is placed only if the repeat echocardiogram shows no improvement. At 47% his fraction is well above the 35% threshold, so therapy continues without a device. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has edema and distended neck veins. Her electrocardiogram shows right axis deviation, right bundle branch block and T-wave inversions in V1 to V3. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Acute lateral infarction","A normal variant tracing","Right heart strain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right axis deviation, right bundle branch block and T-wave inversions in V1 to V3 are the right heart strain pattern, fitting right-sided failure from pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 28%). She is breathless with ordinary exertion but comfortable at rest, and takes no renin-angiotensin drug. Which agent fits her symptom class?","choices":["Dobutamine","Metolazone alone","Sacubitril-valsartan","Diltiazem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with exertion are New York Heart Association class II, and the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III in reduced ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents with fatigue, breathlessness with exertion and new ankle edema. Which blood test best supports a clinical diagnosis of heart failure?","choices":["Serum potassium","B-type natriuretic peptide","Complete blood count","Cardiac troponin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a clinical diagnosis: signs and symptoms plus an elevated natriuretic peptide or objective congestion. The peptide is the laboratory test that carries that weight. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman recovering from acute heart failure after an infarction, with an ejection fraction of 31%, asks why she must wear a defibrillator vest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It is permanent for everyone after infarction","It measures her ejection fraction daily","It protects her while therapy is optimized","It replaces her heart failure medicines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The vest covers the period in which guideline-directed therapy is optimized and she completes cardiac rehabilitation; the echocardiogram is then repeated to decide whether she needs an implantable device. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has progressive symptoms despite treatment of its cause and is not a transplant candidate. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge without follow-up care","Palliative care focused on symptoms","Calcium channel blocker for comfort","Inotropes started for home use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure is treated like left-sided failure, with symptom management, treatment of the cause, heart transplant or palliative care. When transplant is not an option, palliative care is the route. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 46%) is euvolemic on a diuretic. Which drug class is the next best-supported addition?","choices":["Evidence-based beta blocker at once","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Diltiazem for rate and pressure","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After diuretics, the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor carries a moderate recommendation in mildly reduced ejection fraction, ahead of the weak recommendations for the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) on sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and empagliflozin develops atrial fibrillation, and rate control is being planned. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Diltiazem","Spironolactone","Empagliflozin","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem is a calcium channel blocker, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. His existing beta blocker belongs to core therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic left-sided heart failure develops right-sided failure. He asks what this means for his outlook. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["It improves once edema clears","His prognosis is now much worse","His prognosis is unchanged","His prognosis is now better"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure carries a much worse prognosis than left-sided failure, and in him it marks progression of chronic left-sided disease. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has breathlessness, leg edema and a B-type natriuretic peptide of 280. Which study should come next to define his cardiac structure and ejection fraction?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","Cardiac troponin level","Echocardiogram","Repeat the peptide in a year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An indeterminate peptide leaves the question open, and the echocardiogram shows structure most accurately and classifies heart failure by ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with very heavy menstrual bleeding has fatigue, palpitations, ankle edema and a raised B-type natriuretic peptide. Echocardiogram is being arranged. Which additional test is most likely to identify the driver of her heart failure?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","Chest radiograph","Complete blood count","Cardiac troponin level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anemia is one of the causes of high-output failure, and the complete blood count is part of the high-output workup with the echocardiogram, metabolic panel and natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a family history of heart failure smokes, drinks heavily and is obese. Her heart is structurally normal. Which counseling point best lowers her heart failure risk?","choices":["Family history cannot be offset at all","Start a loop diuretic as prevention","Wait for symptoms before changing","Quit tobacco, cut alcohol, lose weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco, excessive alcohol and obesity are modifiable risk factors for heart failure. Her family history is a risk factor she cannot change, so counseling targets the ones she can. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic left ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram. She already takes an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. Which drug class should be added for her diabetes-related risk?","choices":["Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Calcium channel blocker such as amlodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is stage B, and stage B carries a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes as well as the converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
